<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cyprus under Isaac Komnenos</h1><p class="st-meta">1184 CE – 1191 CE · capital Nicosia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breakaway Byzantine realm on Cyprus ruled by the self-proclaimed emperor Isaac Komnenos until Richard the Lionheart conquered the island in 1191.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-komnenos-of-cyprus">Isaac Komnenos</a>, a great-nephew of Emperor Manuel I, had been governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. After his ransom he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1184 with forged imperial letters, took over the government and proclaimed himself emperor, refusing to recognise Andronikos I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Isaac ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and was remembered by Cypriots as harsh and grasping, seizing property and abusing local nobles. He secured his position by allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily">William II of Sicily</a>, whose fleet defeated a Byzantine expedition sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> in 1186-1187, and reportedly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. The island remained Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Greek-speaking.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In May 1191 ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I's</a> sister and his betrothed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-navarre">Berengaria</a> were stranded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a>. Isaac's mistreatment of them gave Richard a pretext: he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> within weeks and held Isaac in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> chains. Richard soon sold the island to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1184 CE – 1191 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.26%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1184 CE – 1191 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicosia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Isaac Komnenos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Komnenian Cyprus, Despotate of Cyprus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=cyprus-under-isaac-komnenos" aria-label="Cyprus under Isaac Komnenos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5525 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cyprus under Isaac Komnenos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5845 1162L5709 970M5845 1162L4970 663M5845 1162L5845 1162"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M4970 663h0M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicosia. <a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=cyprus-under-isaac-komnenos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
